// Joint Library (joint)
//
// PARTS MEET AT INTERFACES, AND FASTENERS ARE INSERTED INTO THEM. Declare each
// interface once — an axis, a face, a channel — and build every part that
// meets there from it; a screw is inserted into an axis, and comes back with
// the parts it joins finished:
//
//   bolt = joint.cylindrical([0.0, 0.0, 10.0], neg(vector.dirZ), 6.0, fastener.fitMedium);
//   screw, lid, base = joint.insert(bolt, joint.socketHead, lidBody, baseBody, 7.5);
//
// is an ISO 4762 M6 cap screw seated at [0, 0, 10] and driven down, its head in
// a DIN 974-1 counterbore and its shank through an ISO 273 medium clearance in
// lidBody, threaded into a tapped hole in baseBody. Change the size or the seat
// and every feature moves with it; nothing is kept in step by hand.
// joint.bolted and joint.countersunk build the same joints from the
// interface's parts passed loose, and joint.planar and joint.slide return a
// face's and a channel's fields directly.
//
// What the joint decides, so the caller does not:
//
// - The FIT, from the series named: the clearance, the counterbore, and the
//   tapped hole at the basic minor diameter, each from the catalog.
// - The SCREW'S LENGTH, from the grip: the shortest ISO 4762 length that
//   threads at least 1.5 diameters into the threaded part, the usual minimum
//   engagement for a steel screw in steel or cast iron.
// - The FINISH of the parts it makes: the screw in steel and the threaded part
//   in steel, each carrying its thread as relief (see fastener's Finishes).
//   The clamped part keeps no finish; set its material on the name it is
//   returned to. A material or colour set on any returned name replaces the
//   joint's.
//
// THE GRIP IS THE CLAMPED PART'S THICKNESS under the head: the distance along
// the shank from the seat to the face where the clamped part meets the
// threaded one. GSL cannot measure a body at build time, so the joint is told,
// and verifyJoints proves it was told right (see Claims).
//
// Conventions follow fastener: `shankDir` is the direction the shank runs from
// the seat, and the seat is where the head bears — the counterbore's floor.
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

// =========================================================
// Interfaces as Values
// =========================================================
//
// AN INTERFACE IS ONE VALUE, declared once and passed to every part that
// meets at it, and a FASTENER IS INSERTED INTO IT:
//
//   bolt = joint.cylindrical([8.0, 26.75, -4.5], vector.dirZ, 3.0, fastener.fitMedium);
//   screw, plate, drum = joint.insert(bolt, joint.socketHead, plateBody, drumBody, 4.0);
//
// GSL has no record type, so an interface is a list of vec4 rows read through
// the accessors below, as a material is. Read it through them: the rows are a
// layout, not an interface. Nothing checks that a value is the kind of
// interface a function takes.

/// The screw heads joint.insert seats: ISO 4762 socket head in a DIN 974-1
/// counterbore, or ISO 10642 flat head flush in a 90-degree countersink.
socketHead = 1.0;
flatHead = 2.0;

/// A CYLINDRICAL interface: an axis through `seatPoint` along `axisDir`, of
/// nominal diameter `nominalDia`, at ISO 273 clearance `fit`. For a screwed
/// joint the seat is where the head bears and the axis runs the way the shank
/// does, as in fastener. START HERE: declare each interface once, at top level,
/// before the parts, and build every part that meets there from it — insert a
/// screw into an axis with joint.insert, bound the parts on either side of a
/// face with joint.planarFace, cut a guide with joint.prismatic:
///
///   bolt = joint.cylindrical([8.0, 26.75, -4.5], vector.dirZ, 3.0, fastener.fitMedium);
///   screw, plate, drum = joint.insert(bolt, joint.socketHead, plateBody, drumBody, 12.0);
cylindrical(seatPoint, axisDir, nominalDia, fit) {
  u = normalize(axisDir);
  return [[seatPoint.x, seatPoint.y, seatPoint.z, nominalDia], [u.x, u.y, u.z, fit]];
}

/// A cylindrical interface's seat point.
seat(iface) {
  r = iface[0];
  return r.xyz;
}

/// A cylindrical interface's unit axis: the direction its shank runs from the
/// seat.
// Not axis(): builtin's axis(center, direction) is reached unqualified here.
direction(iface) {
  r = iface[1];
  return r.xyz;
}

/// A cylindrical interface's nominal diameter.
diameter(iface) {
  r = iface[0];
  return r.w;
}

/// A cylindrical interface's ISO 273 clearance series.
fit(iface) {
  r = iface[1];
  return r.w;
}

/// A FASTENER INSERTED into cylindrical interface `iface`: a `head` screw
/// (joint.socketHead or joint.flatHead) clamping `clamped`, `grip` thick, to
/// `threaded`. Returns the screw, then `clamped` and `threaded` with their
/// features cut, as joint.bolted and joint.countersunk do — the same parts,
/// lengths, finishes and claims, the head chosen by the constant.
insert(iface, head, clamped, threaded, grip) {
  seatPoint = seat(iface);
  shankDir = direction(iface);
  nominalDia = diameter(iface);
  fitSeries = fit(iface);
  at =fastener.seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir);
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  len = head == flatHead ? countersunkLength(nominalDia, grip) : boltedLength(nominalDia, grip);
  tapDepth = len + 3.0 * pitchVal;
  // a flat head's thread starts below the head; a socket head's at the seat
  threadTop = head == flatHead ? 0.0 - (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0 : 0.0;

  // The finishes are set here, not taken from joint.bolted or
  // joint.countersunk: a property is carried out of one function call, not two.
  screw = remap(head == flatHead ? fastener.countersunk(nominalDia, len) : fastener.shcs(nominalDia, len), at);
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
                     remap(thread.maleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, threadTop, 0.0 - len), at));
  screw.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor,
                  remap(thread.maleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, threadTop, 0.0 - len), at), pitchVal);

  held = max(clamped, neg(remap(head == flatHead ? fastener.countersunkHole(nominalDia, fitSeries)
                                                 : fastener.shcsHole(nominalDia, fitSeries), at)));

  tapped = max(threaded, neg(remap(fastener.tappedHole(nominalDia, len), at)));
  tapped.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
                      remap(thread.femaleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip, 0.0 - tapDepth), at));
  tapped.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor,
                   remap(thread.femaleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip, 0.0 - tapDepth), at),
                   pitchVal);
  return screw, held, tapped;
}

/// A PLANAR interface: the face through `point` with unit `normal`, where one
/// part sits on another. joint.above and joint.below bound the two parts, as
/// joint.planar's two returns do:
///
///   face = joint.planarFace([0.0, 0.0, -7.5], vector.dirZ);
///   plate = max(plateBlank, joint.above(face));
///   cover = max(coverBlank, joint.below(face));
planarFace(point, normal) {
  u = normalize(normal);
  return [[point.x, point.y, point.z, 0.0], [u.x, u.y, u.z, 0.0]];
}

/// The half-space on a planar interface's normal side, negative there.
above(face) {
  r0 = face[0];
  r1 = face[1];
  return neg(dot(p - r0.xyz, r1.xyz));
}

/// The half-space behind a planar interface, negative there.
below(face) {
  r0 = face[0];
  r1 = face[1];
  return dot(p - r0.xyz, r1.xyz);
}

/// A PRISMATIC interface: joint.slide's channel as one value — along
/// `axisDir` through `point`, `width` by `height` in section with `upDir` up,
/// at running `clearance`. joint.channel and joint.envelope are joint.slide's
/// two returns:
///
///   guide = joint.prismatic([0.0, 17.5, -4.0], vector.dirX, vector.dirZ, 6.0, 5.0, 0.3);
///   plate = max(plateBlank, neg(joint.channel(guide)));
///   rail = max(railBlank, joint.envelope(guide));
prismatic(point, axisDir, upDir, width, height, clearance) {
  return [[point.x, point.y, point.z, width], [axisDir.x, axisDir.y, axisDir.z, height],
          [upDir.x, upDir.y, upDir.z, clearance]];
}

/// The channel to cut from a prismatic interface's guide.
channel(guide) {
  r0 = guide[0];
  r1 = guide[1];
  r2 = guide[2];
  c, e = slide(r0.xyz, r1.xyz, r2.xyz, r0.w, r1.w, r2.w);
  return c;
}

/// The envelope a prismatic interface's running part fits in.
envelope(guide) {
  r0 = guide[0];
  r1 = guide[1];
  r2 = guide[2];
  c, e = slide(r0.xyz, r1.xyz, r2.xyz, r0.w, r1.w, r2.w);
  return e;
}

// =========================================================
// Bolted
// =========================================================

/// The ISO 4762 length a bolted joint of this size and grip uses: the
/// shortest catalog length that runs 1.5 diameters past the grip. Zero if no
/// catalog screw is that long.
boltedLength(nominalDia, grip) {
  return catalog.shcsLength(grip + 1.5 * nominalDia);
}

/// A BOLTED JOINT: an ISO 4762 cap screw seated at `seatPoint` with its shank
/// running along `shankDir`, clamping `clamped` (`grip` thick under the head)
/// to `threaded`, at ISO 273 clearance `fit` (fastener.fitFine, fitMedium or
/// fitCoarse). Returns the screw, then `clamped` with its counterbore and
/// clearance cut, then `threaded` with its tapped hole cut, each a part of its
/// own:
///
///   screw, lid, base = joint.bolted(lidBody, baseBody, seat, shankDir, 6.0, 7.5,
///                                   fastener.fitMedium);
///   lidMat, lidColor = metal.aluminum();
///   lid.material = lidMat;
///   return screw, lid, base;
///
/// The holes are open above and the clearance is a thru, so each part's own
/// faces bound them: the counterbore opens through whatever of `clamped` is
/// above the seat. Chain joints by passing one joint's returned parts to the
/// next.
bolted(clamped, threaded, seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip, fit) {
  at = fastener.seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir);
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  len = boltedLength(nominalDia, grip);
  tapDepth = len + 3.0 * pitchVal;

  // remap(shcs, at), not shcsSeated: shcsSeated's box is right only for a
  // shank along z, and a joint goes square to any face.
  screw = remap(fastener.shcs(nominalDia, len), at);
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
                     remap(thread.maleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0, 0.0 - len), at));
  screw.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor,
                  remap(thread.maleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0, 0.0 - len), at), pitchVal);

  held = max(clamped, neg(remap(fastener.shcsHole(nominalDia, fit), at)));

  tapped = max(threaded, neg(remap(fastener.tappedHole(nominalDia, len), at)));
  tapped.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
                      remap(thread.femaleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip, 0.0 - tapDepth), at));
  tapped.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor,
                   remap(thread.femaleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip, 0.0 - tapDepth), at),
                   pitchVal);
  return screw, held, tapped;
}

// =========================================================
// Countersunk
// =========================================================

/// The ISO 10642 length a countersunk joint of this size and grip uses: the
/// shortest catalog length that runs 1.5 diameters past the grip. A flat head's
/// length is overall, its head inside the grip. Zero if no catalog screw is
/// that long.
countersunkLength(nominalDia, grip) {
  return catalog.cskLength(grip + 1.5 * nominalDia);
}

/// A COUNTERSUNK JOINT: an ISO 10642 flat head screw seated flush at
/// `seatPoint` — the countersink's rim, on the clamped part's outer face — its
/// shank running along `shankDir`, clamping `clamped` (`grip` thick from that
/// face to the face it meets `threaded` on) to `threaded`, at ISO 273
/// clearance `fit`. Returns the screw, then `clamped` with its 90-degree
/// countersink and clearance cut, then `threaded` with its tapped hole cut:
///
///   screw, cover, plate = joint.countersunk(coverBody, plateBody, seat, shankDir, 3.0, 2.0,
///                                           fastener.fitMedium);
///
/// The countersink's rim is the head's theoretical sharp diameter, so head
/// and countersink coincide at maximum material. A clamped part thinner than
/// the head is deep — (dk - d) / 2, 1.86 for an M3 — sinks the head into the
/// threaded part, and verifyJoints reports the screw overlapping it outside
/// its thread.
countersunk(clamped, threaded, seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip, fit) {
  at = fastener.seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir);
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  len = countersunkLength(nominalDia, grip);
  tapDepth = len + 3.0 * pitchVal;
  headDepth = (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0;

  screw = remap(fastener.countersunk(nominalDia, len), at);
  steelMat, steelColor = metal.steel();
  screw.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
                     remap(thread.maleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - headDepth, 0.0 - len), at));
  screw.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor,
                  remap(thread.maleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - headDepth, 0.0 - len), at), pitchVal);

  held = max(clamped, neg(remap(fastener.countersunkHole(nominalDia, fit), at)));

  tapped = max(threaded, neg(remap(fastener.tappedHole(nominalDia, len), at)));
  tapped.material = material.withDisplacement(steelMat,
                      remap(thread.femaleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip, 0.0 - tapDepth), at));
  tapped.color = thread.reliefColor(steelColor,
                   remap(thread.femaleRelief(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip, 0.0 - tapDepth), at),
                   pitchVal);
  return screw, held, tapped;
}

// =========================================================
// Declared Interfaces
// =========================================================
//
// A FACE ONE PART SITS ON, OR A CHANNEL ONE PART RUNS IN, cuts nothing a
// fastener would: the interface is a datum both parts are built from. These
// name the datum once and hand back the fields that bound each side of it, so
// the two parts cannot disagree about where they meet. They carry no claims of
// their own; a gate or verifyMechanism grades what they make.

/// A PLANAR interface: the face through `point` with unit `normal`, where one
/// part sits on another. Returns the field bounding the part on the normal's
/// side, then the one bounding the part behind it:
///
///   above, below = joint.planar([0.0, 0.0, -7.5], vector.dirZ);
///   plate = max(plateBlank, above);   // the plate's underside is the face
///   cover = max(coverBlank, below);   // and so is the cover's top
///
/// Each is a half-space, negative on its own side, so a blank that reaches the
/// face is cut flat on it and neither part can cross it.
planar(point, normal) {
  d = dot(p - point, normal);
  return neg(d), d;
}

/// A PRISMATIC interface: a straight channel along `axisDir` through `point`,
/// `width` across (along the cross of `upDir` and `axisDir`) and `height` along
/// `upDir`, for a part `width` by `height` in section to run in at `clearance`
/// all round. Returns the channel to cut from the guide, then the envelope the
/// running part's section fits in, both unbounded along the axis:
///
///   channel, envelope = joint.slide([0.0, 17.5, -4.0], vector.dirX, vector.dirZ, 6.0, 5.0, 0.3);
///   plate = max(plateBlank, neg(channel));
///   rail = max(railBlank, envelope);
///
/// The clearance is the running clearance the design declares — the play the
/// part has in its guide — not an ISO 286 fit.
slide(point, axisDir, upDir, width, height, clearance) {
  q = p - point;
  across = normalize(cross(upDir, axisDir));
  u = abs(dot(q, across));
  v = abs(dot(q, upDir));
  channel = max(u - (width / 2.0 + clearance), v - (height / 2.0 + clearance));
  envelope = max(u - width / 2.0, v - height / 2.0);
  return channel, envelope;
}

// =========================================================
// Claims
// =========================================================
//
// WHAT A BOLTED JOINT HAS TO BE TRUE, as fields a certify proves are never
// negative over the joint's region. verifyJoints finds every joint.bolted call
// and proves these for it; they are here so a proof by hand reads the same.
// With the call's own arguments and the names its parts went to:
//
//   clears the clamped part      max(screw, lid, joint.boltedSeat(seat, dir, d, fit))
//   threads only where engaged   max(screw, base, joint.boltedEngaged(seat, dir, d, grip))
//   the grip is clamped metal    max(joint.boltedGripZone(seat, dir, d, grip, fit), neg(lidBody))
//   the thread has metal         max(joint.boltedThreadZone(seat, dir, d, grip), neg(baseBody))
//
// and against everything else in the assembly, the screw with both
// allowances. The first two are the joint's own fit and hold by construction
// at nominal; the last two are the CONTEXT — whether the grip it was told is
// the grip the parts have, and whether there is a wall around the thread —
// and are what a design actually gets wrong.

// jointZ: the coordinate along the joint's axis, zero on the seat and positive
// on the head's side.
jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir) {
  return neg(dot(p - seatPoint, shankDir));
}

// jointR: the distance from the joint's axis.
jointR(seatPoint, shankDir) {
  q = p - seatPoint;
  return length(q - shankDir * dot(q, shankDir));
}

/// The SEAT ALLOWANCE: POSITIVE in a band 0.1 either side of the seating
/// plane, out to the counterbore's radius. The head bears on the counterbore's
/// floor, which is contact, not interference; take the max of the screw and a
/// part with this and the floor is let through and nothing else is.
boltedSeat(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, fit) {
  return min(0.1 - abs(jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir)),
             catalog.counterbore(nominalDia) / 2.0 + 0.1 - jointR(seatPoint, shankDir));
}

/// The ENGAGEMENT ALLOWANCE: positive where the screw's thread and the tapped
/// hole's are both cut, grown by 0.2 — thread.engaged for this joint. The
/// smooth screw at its major diameter overlaps the tapped part at its minor
/// there by design. The margin is twice thread.engaged's usual 0.1 so that a
/// joint on a tilted axis, whose intervals are looser, still proves in a
/// default budget; the threads are the same size and pitch by construction,
/// so the wider band lets nothing through that a mismatch would have made.
boltedEngaged(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip) {
  at = fastener.seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir);
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  len = boltedLength(nominalDia, grip);
  return thread.engaged(remap(thread.maleZone(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0, 0.0 - len), at),
                        remap(thread.femaleZone(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip,
                                                0.0 - (len + 3.0 * pitchVal)), at),
                        0.2);
}

/// The GRIP ZONE, negative inside: the ring under the head, from the
/// clearance out to the head's own diameter, and along the axis the grip inset
/// from both its faces by the slack (see jointSlack). The clamped part has to
/// fill it — prove max(zone, neg(clampedBody)) is never negative — or the head
/// bears on nothing, or the grip the joint was told is not the parts' grip.
boltedGripZone(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip, fit) {
  r = jointR(seatPoint, shankDir);
  z = jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir);
  slack = jointSlack(grip);
  return max(catalog.clearance(nominalDia, fit) / 2.0 + 0.1 - r,
             r - catalog.shcsHeadDia(nominalDia) / 2.0,
             z + slack, 0.0 - grip + slack - z);
}

/// The THREAD ZONE, negative inside: a cylinder three quarters of a diameter
/// in radius — a quarter-diameter wall outside the thread's crest — from the
/// slack past the grip to 1.5 diameters past it. The threaded part has to fill
/// it, or the thread breaks out of a wall or engages less than 1.5 d.
boltedThreadZone(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip) {
  z = jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir);
  return max(jointR(seatPoint, shankDir) - 0.75 * nominalDia,
             z + grip + jointSlack(grip), 0.0 - grip - 1.5 * nominalDia - z);
}

// THE COUNTERSUNK JOINT'S CLAIMS are the bolted joint's with its own seat,
// engagement and grip zone; the thread zone is the same (boltedThreadZone).

// cskCone: the distance outward from the countersink's cone, the head's
// theoretical sharp surface, negative inside it: a 90-degree cone losing a
// millimetre of radius per millimetre of depth below the seat.
cskCone(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia) {
  return (jointR(seatPoint, shankDir)
          - (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) / 2.0 + jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir))) * 0.7071;
}

/// The countersunk SEAT ALLOWANCE: POSITIVE within 0.1 of the cone the head
/// and the countersink share, from the rim to the head's depth. They coincide
/// at maximum material, which is contact, not interference.
countersunkSeat(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia) {
  z = jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir);
  depth = (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0;
  return min(0.1 - abs(cskCone(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia)), 0.1 - z, z + depth + 0.1);
}

/// The countersunk ENGAGEMENT ALLOWANCE: boltedEngaged for the ISO 10642
/// length this joint picks.
countersunkEngaged(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip) {
  at = fastener.seatedAt(seatPoint, shankDir);
  pitchVal = thread.metricPitch(nominalDia);
  len = countersunkLength(nominalDia, grip);
  return thread.engaged(remap(thread.maleZone(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0, 0.0 - len), at),
                        remap(thread.femaleZone(nominalDia, pitchVal, 0.0 - grip,
                                                0.0 - (len + 3.0 * pitchVal)), at),
                        0.2);
}

/// The countersunk GRIP ZONE, negative inside: a shell 0.1 to 0.6 outside the
/// head's cone, clear of the clearance hole, from the slack below the rim to the
/// slack above the head's depth. The clamped part has to fill it — the head is
/// held all round its cone — or the countersink breaks out of an edge. (A
/// clamped part thinner than the head is deep is the engagement claim's to
/// catch: the head sinks into the threaded part.) Below the head the grip is the
/// clamped part's own business: a countersunk head bears on its cone, not on a
/// face under it.
countersunkGripZone(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia, grip, fit) {
  c = cskCone(seatPoint, shankDir, nominalDia);
  z = jointZ(seatPoint, shankDir);
  depth = (catalog.cskHeadDia(nominalDia) - nominalDia) / 2.0;
  slack = jointSlack(grip);
  return max(0.1 - c, c - 0.6,
             catalog.clearance(nominalDia, fit) / 2.0 + 0.1 - jointR(seatPoint, shankDir),
             z + slack, 0.0 - depth + slack - z);
}

// jointSlack: how far the zones stand in from the grip's faces, 0.5 or a
// quarter of the grip if that is less. It is how wrong the grip may be before
// a claim fails, and it is what keeps the proof cheap: a zone flush with a
// face leaves a slab as thin as the inset across the zone's whole section, and
// an interval proof has to resolve all of it.
jointSlack(grip) {
  return min(0.5, grip / 4.0);
}
